Protocol Reference: Server Message Block (SMB)
Overview
SMB (Server Message Block) is a network file sharing protocol that allows applications on a computer to read and write to files and request services from programs in a computer network.

Ports Used:

Port 445 (TCP): Modern SMB running directly over TCP/IP (microsoft-ds).

Port 139 (TCP): Legacy SMB running over NetBIOS Session Service.

Common Share Types
Administrative Shares (C$, ADMIN$): Hidden default shares used by Windows administrators. Access typically requires admin credentials.

IPC$ (Inter-Process Communication): Used for communication between processes via named pipes.

User/Custom Shares: Directories explicitly shared by administrators (e.g., WorkShares).

Command Cheat Sheet
Network Reconnaissance
Bash
# Test connectivity
ping <TARGET_IP>

# Service version scan on standard ports
nmap -sV -sC <TARGET_IP>

SMB Enumeration & Access (smbclient)
Bash
# List available shares on the target (anonymous / null session)
smbclient -L <TARGET_IP>

# Connect to a specific share as a Guest / Anonymous user
smbclient //<TARGET_IP>/WorkShares -U Guest

# When prompted for a password, press Enter (blank password)

Navigating the Interactive SMB Shell
Bash
smb: \> ls                     # List files and directories in current folder
smb: \> cd DirectoryName        # Change directory
smb: \> get flag.txt           # Download a file to local working directory
smb: \> mget *.txt             # Download multiple files matching pattern
smb: \> help                   # Show list of available SMB commands
smb: \> exit                   # Close SMB connection  
